  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Testing a Combined SIT/IIT Approach to Control Mosquito-Borne Diseases at Scale</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mosquito-borne diseases cause suffering for hundreds of millions of people and claim more than 700,000 lives yearly. Diseases such as dengue, Zika, and chikungunya cause symptoms similar to malaria and are endemic in many parts of the world, yet there are no treatments for them nor vaccines for Zika and chikungunya. Mosquito control, particularly of the Aedes aegypti species, is seen as a potentially effective solution to slow or stop the spread of these diseases but has not yet demonstrated significant, sustainable impacts on disease transmission. Other than the application of toxic pesticides, there are currently two main forms of mosquito control: Sterile Insect Technique (SIT), where males are sterilized using radiation, and Incompatible Insect Technique (IIT), where males are infected with the Wolbachia bacteria, leaving wild females unable to reproduce when they mate. Neither technique has any negative human health effects; Wolbachia naturally occurs in insects but is not dangerous to humans. Individually, these strategies show promising results in suppressing the mosquito population, but neither has been shown to control mosquito-borne diseases sufficiently. However, SIT and IIT can potentially complement each other. Researchers from Michigan State University (MSU), in partnership with the Universidad Autnoma de Yucatn (UADY), are now testing this combined approach in Mexicos Yucatn Peninsula. With support from Development Innovation Ventures, MSU will implement this bundled approach and measure changes in mosquito population density and the prevalence of mosquito-borne diseases in humans. This investment builds on past USAID support through the Combating Zika and Future Threats Grand Challenge, which partially funded the UADY mosquito mass-rearing facility in 2016. This research will generate evidence of the cost-effectiveness of the combined SIT/IIT approach and its ability to address these otherwise untreatable diseases, which will provide importa</narrative>
  </description>
